<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Martinique</h1><p class="st-meta">1635 CE – 1946 CE · capital Saint-Pierre; later Fort-Royal (Fort-de-France)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French Caribbean sugar colony, birthplace of the Empress Joséphine and later of the négritude movement, which became an overseas department of France in 1946.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-pierre-martinique">Saint-Pierre</a> in 1635 and claimed the island for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-des-iles-de-lamerique">Compagnie des Îles d'Amérique</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> were pushed to the east and then expelled by 1660. Martinique came under direct royal rule in 1674 and became the seat of the governor-general of the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Planter society was built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> estates worked by enslaved Africans under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-noir">Code Noir</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-pierre-martinique">Saint-Pierre</a> was the commercial and cultural centre, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-de-france">Fort-Royal</a>, later Fort-de-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, held the administration. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> occupied Martinique several times, notably during 1794 to 1802, which kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in force there during the revolutionary abolition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephine-de-beauharnais">Joséphine de Beauharnais</a> was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-trois-ilets">Trois-Îlets</a> in 1763.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> ended in 1848 after a rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-pierre-martinique">Saint-Pierre</a>. The eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pelee">Mount Pelée</a> in 1902 destroyed Saint-Pierre and killed about 28,000 people. Under a 1946 law championed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a>, Martinique became an overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, as it remains.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1635 CE – 1946 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.24%;width:6.19%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1635 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Pierre; later Fort-Royal (Fort-de-France)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc, Jacques Dyel du Parquet</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French and Martinican Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Martinique, Martinique</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1790&amp;focus=colony-of-martinique" aria-label="Colony of Martinique on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3360 534 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Martinique lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3680 790L505